    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\nLargest Boeing 737 MAX Cleared to Fly With Updates to the Systems That Caused Two Crashes\n11 photos\nPhoto: Boeing\nThey call it the 737-10, and it is the newest and largest version of the Boeing 737 MAX family. How large would that be? Think 230 passengers in maximum seating configuration, something that, for a single-aisle airplane, is quite something.\nAs you know, the 737 MAX has a pretty troubled history, with two crashes that occurred back in 2018 and 2019 leading to the entire fleet that existed at that time being grounded all over the world between March 2019 and December 2020.\nThat hasn't stopped Boeing in its tracks, though, and the company has been hard at work making the planes as safe as they can be. This new version is the first to incorporate some of these changes right off the factory floor.\nThe 737-10 is the latest addition to the family, joining the existing 737-7, 737-8, 737-8-200, and 737-9. What makes it different, aside from the revised safety systems we'll discuss later on, is that fact that it carry the largest number of passenger.\nPhoto: Boeing Technically speaking, operators can spec the plane to be able to move between 185 and 230 passengers, seated in two classes. The maximum range the aircraft can reach is 3,566 miles (5,740 km), making it ideal for use on some of the most popular routes. Also, that's 420 miles (676 km) more than what competitor planes without auxiliary fuel tanks offer.\nEarlier this week, Boeing said the plane finally got its Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) certification (after several years of delays), something that clears it to fly in American skies and thus opens the doors for Boeing to start making deliveries and please both airlines and customers – supposedly, the plane will offer the lowest per-seat costs of any single-aisle airplane currently in operation.\nTo get here, says the company, a total of around 1,000 flight tests have been performed, totaling more than 2,000 flight hours, but also 1,000 hours of ground tests. At the end of it all, the 737-10 became the first commercial airplane to be certified to the provisions of the harsher 2020 Aircraft Certification, Safety, and Accountability Act (ACSAA).\nPerhaps the most important thing about the plane is that it incorporates a solution that should address the issue that led to the crash of Lion Air Flight 610 and Ethiopian Airlines Flight 302, which took 346 lives. That would be, above all, an enhanced Angle-of-Attack system (AoA).\nPhoto: Boeing The crashes mentioned above were caused by one of the two AoA sensors fitted on the outside of the planes feeding wrong data to the plane's Maneuvering Characteristics Augmentation System (MCAS). As a result, the tech continuously pitched the nose of the planes down, regardless of the efforts made by the pilots to correct it.\nBoeing now says that the AoAs on the 737-10 benefit from no less than five monitoring systems that track the way the sensors operate at takeoff, after takeoff, and in case they go through a bird strike. If an error is detected, the system steps in and prevents it from propagating to other systems that might impact the safety of the flight.\nThe error will be communicated to the pilots through an AoA Fail alert shown on the primary displays, accompanied by details about which of the two sensors is responsible for the error. This, says Boeing, also eliminates the pilots' need to go through an extensive checklist so that they could identify the problem.\nAs a sort of redundancy system, the cockpit now comes with a physical switch pilots can use to turn off the stick shaker, the tech that lets pilots know their aircraft is approaching a stall condition.\nPhoto: Boeing In the two crashes caused by the AoA and MCAS, the sensor error propagated to the stick shaker as well, making pilots believe they're rapidly approaching the moment their plane will fall from the sky – something that complicated matters even more for the ones involved.\nDespite the troubles the 737 Max went through, the industry doesn't seem too scared. When it comes to the 737-10 alone, for instance, there are some 1,555 planes waiting to be delivered, which amount to around 30 percent of the order book for the entire family, which stands at 7,350 orders.\nBoeing says the revised sensors and the other modifications made to the 737-10 will be retrofitted on other models of the family already in service, but does not mention how many planes that is, or when the retrofitting will begin. All other versions to be produced from now on will incorporate the upgrades as well.\nAs for the cost of one of these new aircraft , that, naturally, depends on a lot of factors. The starting price for the 737 MAX is of around $100 million, but the final cost can be, in some cases, some 30 percent higher than that.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBoeing boeing 737 Boeing 737 MAX airplane\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Go-Future Trike Concept Is How One British Designer Envisions Us Shopping – Built With Plastic",
